    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a decent start, but it feels a bit surface-level. Could you go deeper into the originalist side? I'm interested in the specific historical and linguistic evidence they use to ground their arguments, more so than the living constitutionalist side which seems to be more about general principles.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly. Originalists, such as Scalia, delve deeply into historical context when interpreting the Second Amendment. They examine the Federalist Papers, particularly those addressing concerns over standing armies and the raising of militia, as evidence that the framers intended for an armed populace to deter tyranny. They critically assess the 18th-century societal baseline, noting the prevalence of personal, family, and home defense issues. Linguistically, originalists focus on the original spelling of the words in the Amendment — \"the people\" and not \"the citizenry\" — arguing that this allows for a broader interpretation of \"militias\" to include organized common defense, notably private defense. Originalists also appraise historical Supreme Court decisions, such as United States v. Miller (1939), to demonstrate the entrenched role of regulated militias in the national defense. Originalists consistent interpret the text as it was understood at the time it was written, firmly believing that any evolution in meaning should be addressed through formal constitutional amendments rather than judicial interpretation.",
      "source": "target",
