    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a very detailed breakdown of the originalist position. It sounds like they have a very consistent, evidence-based framework they use. Can you do the same for the living constitutionalist side? I'd like to see if they have similarly rigorous legal or historical foundations, or if their arguments are primarily based on modern policy preferences.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Living constitutionalists also rely on a rigorous foundation, though their approach focuses on the broader application of the Constitution's principles to societal evolution. They reference a multitude of sources, from historical documents and scholarly writings to contemporary social science research, to show how the Framers' intentions were to create enduring protections against governmental overreach. Politically, they often look to figures like James Madison, who wrote about the need for Congress to enable the people to resist oppression, as evidence of the enduring principles behind the Second Amendment.\n\n\nFor linguistic arguments, living constitutionalists might examine changes in American society to illustrate the modern meaning of \"the right of the people to keep and bear Arms.\" They highlight the post-Civil War Reconstruction era, noting the amendment's role in the struggle for racial equality, pointing to a broader and more inclusive understanding of \"the right of the people.\" Scholars like Professor Akhil Reed Amar, Charles Geyh, and Mark Kelman have provided historical, linguistic, and theoretical foundations demonstrating the Second Amendment's application to contemporary forms of arms.\n\n\nLiving constitutionalists point to a wealth of case law from the Supreme Court, where they've championed interpretations that acknowledge the right to bear arms for self-defense beyond strictly organized militia usage, like in District of Columbia",
      "source": "target",
